WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.120
For every acclaimed action movie, there are
a dozen more that were shredded by critics.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.360
In fairness, a lot of those films probably
deserve their lousy Rotten Tomatoes scores.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.740
After all, making an action movie is no easy
task, and you need more than a couple of explosions

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.020
to create an awesome film.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.340
On the other hand, Rotten Tomatoes is a website
that lacks nuance, and sometimes, you might

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.260
find an adrenaline-laced gem under all those
slimy green splotches.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.900
From films with monstrous snakes to gigantic
gods, these action movies might have terrible

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.460
Tomatometers, but they're still worth watching.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.660
Plagued by production woes and a whitewashing
controversy, The Lone Ranger was in trouble

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:37.560
from day one.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.660
And granted, Gore Verbinski's film is a mess,
but in a way, that's what makes it so fascinating.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.540
There are so many ideas at play here, from
the exploitation of Chinese laborers and the

00:00:47.549 --> 00:00:53.159
genocide of Native Americans to the takeover
of American politics by corrupt corporations.

00:00:53.159 --> 00:00:57.329
The cinematography is sweeping and beautiful,
and some of the sets feel like they were taken

00:00:57.329 --> 00:00:58.640
from a Baz Luhrmann movie.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.299
While the decision to cast Johnny Depp as
Tonto may have been culturally insensitive,

00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:07.670
he is actually hilarious, and he has real
chemistry with the charming Armie Hammer.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.620
"If you could just point us in the right direction,
we'll take it from here.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:12.160
What's that?

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:13.960
Nothing!"

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:22.310
While it's no Pirates of the Caribbean, The
Lone Ranger doesn't deserve its bad reputation

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.160
and proves even the messiest movies have a
silver lining.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:31.380
Set in the Amazon jungle, Anaconda is a big,
dumb monster flick that traffics in B-movie

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.080
scares, and we mean that in the best possible
way.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.979
It follows a group of documentary filmmakers,
led by Jennifer Lopez, who run into the world's

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.749
biggest and hungriest snake.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.479
This creature yanks its victims underwater,
snatches them out of the air, and never seems

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.300
to get its fill of human flesh.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:53.039
And while it's occasionally done with silly
CG, there's also a fantastic animatronic snake

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.220
that looks like it escaped from an evil Rainforest
Cafe.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.180
But the serpent isn't even the highlight of
the movie.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.359
The real draw is Jon Voight as a smarmy poacher
with an untraceable accent.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.040
He leers and sneers every second he's on camera,
all the while plotting ways to catch the killer

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.460
reptile, even if that means using human bait.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.340
You should just ignore the cynical critics
who panned Anaconda, because they're just

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.500
trying to squeeze all the fun out of cinema.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:32.220
2006's Tokyo Drift is the black sheep of the
Fast and Furious franchise.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:37.189
Paul Walker is nowhere to be found, Vin Diesel
only shows up for a few seconds, and the undercover

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:41.420
cops and international heists are replaced
by a soapy teenage melodrama.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.430
The plot follows a troubled American kid named
Sean who moves to Japan, gets involved with

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:50.840
the yakuza, and is forced to race the local
bully to save his buddies, his girlfriend,

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.920
and his own honor.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.820
Of course, before he can become the king of
the road, this hotshot has to learn how to

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:56.320
drift.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.400
In other words, it's The Karate Kid with cars.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:10.060
Watching these hot rods drift their way down
mountain roads is pretty impressive, and even

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:10.680
kind of beautiful.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:16.060
On top of all that, Tokyo Drift introduces
some key players to the F&amp;F franchise: actor

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:19.519
Sung Kang, director Justin Lin, and writer
Chris Morgan.

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:23.440
These guys would be instrumental in shaping
the series going forward, but even as its

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.769
own movie, Tokyo Drift is a slick-looking
film with fun race sequences and characters

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:28.300
we care about.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.180
Guy Ritchie is best known for making comedic
crime thrillers, which would seem to make

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.480
him a weird pick to direct King Arthur: Legend
of the Sword.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.700
And admittedly, there are parts of this film
that are a chore to sit through.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:53.320
But you can't deny its ambition, especially
when the monsters roll onto the screen.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:58.660
There are war elephants that tower over castles,
and then we watch as a mass of writhing tentacles

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.880
reveal slimy witches that are part woman,
part kraken.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.320
"We kept our side of the covenant.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.250
It was you who lost the boy, and the sword."

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:09.000
"Have I not sacrificed enough?!"

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.020
But there's more going on here than just high
fantasy.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.840
Ritchie brings his trademark humor to the
film, and almost every character acts like

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.580
a cockney criminal, making King Arthur: Legend
of the Sword a wonderfully weird way to spend

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:21.600
a Saturday afternoon.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.300
"Now tell me a story about a girl called Lucy,
a viking called Greybeard, and some rebel

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.280
graffiti dirtying these walls."

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.260
"Are you writin' a book?"

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.100
Critics hated Bright.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:32.600
In fact, they loathed it.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:33.420
And you know what?

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.540
It's totally understandable.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:39.520
Bright has a lot of problems, from its lazy
worldbuilding and sloppy screenplay to its

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.340
awkward handling of racial issues.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:46.270
But while it doesn't live up to its potential,
it's nevertheless filled with striking images,

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.280
like a centaur cop and a dragon flying over
Los Angeles.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.660
And while the story mechanics and character
arcs are messy, David Ayer knows how to direct

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:54.420
an action scene.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.360
The gas station shootout is a highlight, and
the moment when Will Smith saves his orc partner

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.880
from a gang of murderous cops is wonderfully
done.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.900
But the best thing about Bright has got to
be Joel Edgerton, who plays the first orc

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.920
ever hired to work for the LAPD.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:13.280
He wonderfully conveys pain underneath all
his prosthetics, and you should check out

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.740
Bright for his performance alone.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.860
"He fulfilled a great prophecy, Ward.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:20.600
I think we might be in a prophecy."

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:26.020
Universal Studios has been trying to create
a monstrous cinematic universe for quite some

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:26.520
time.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:31.740
Before The Mummy completely unraveled in 2017,
the studio was hoping to get its "Dark Universe"

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.320
going with Dracula Untold in 2014.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.760
Unfortunately, lousy box office numbers and
a poor Tomatometer forced the studio to stake

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:40.740
this film in the heart.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.300
But while it's not a great movie by any stretch
of the imagination, Dracula Untold is wonderfully

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:46.940
dumb fun.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.720
It's basically a vampire movie meets a superhero
flick.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.800
Directed by Gary Shore, Dracula Untold finds
Luke Evans as the titular bloodsucker.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:04.940
Only instead of trying to murder innocent
women, this Dracula is an undead superhero,

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.540
trying to protect his homeland from invading
Turks.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:13.400
This dark tale truly shines when Dracula is
beating up bad guys: He plows into a platoon

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.300
of goons like a mad NFL player, all while
roaring like a lion.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:21.840
And using Force-like powers, he commands a
legion of bats to batter his enemies.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:26.740
He even creates his own vampire army, resulting
in a brilliantly bloody climax.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:31.270
This isn't high art, but if you've ever wondered
what would happen if Dracula used his bat-powers

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:32.900
for good, then this is the movie for you.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:34.640
"That's why you got sick, isn't it?

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:36.400
To save me?"

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:38.820
"Yes.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.020
And I would do it all again."

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:49.320
Directed by James Wan, Death Sentence has
the gore of Saw, the scares of The Conjuring,

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.420
and the family feels of Furious 7.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:55.300
In fact, we're legitimately shocked that it
has such a low Tomatometer.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.120
Unlike most of the films on this list, there
are no obvious reasons why it was blasted

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.180
so hard by the critics.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.520
Instead, this 2007 revenge thriller is gritty,
gross, and utterly compelling.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:18.360
With its opening montage of home video footage,
we're rooting for Nick Hume from frame one.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:24.300
He's a good dad and a loving husband, so naturally,
he's devastated when his oldest son is murdered

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.200
by a machete-wielding gangster.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.820
When Nick realizes the legal system is going
to let the killer off easy, he takes justice

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:31.920
into his own own hands.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:36.810
But what starts off as a simple act of revenge
spirals into a full-on war when the criminal's

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:38.240
brother comes back for blood.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.940
Kevin Bacon is key to the movie's success.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.600
He's not some Charles Bronson-super vigilante.

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:46.400
He's a real human being struggling with guilt
and grief.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:50.840
He barely knows how to use a gun, and he breaks
down into tears after murdering his son's

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:51.340
killer.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:55.900
You really feel the love this guy has for
his family and his hatred for the thugs who've

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:56.860
threatened his home.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.360
In addition to Bacon, there's John Goodman
as an f-bomb-dropping force of nature who

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.220
sells Nick his cache of weapons in the best
gun-buying scene since Taxi Driver.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.480
"Dad to dad, don't tell me about it.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.640
Just, go do it."

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:19.340
It all caps off with one of cinema's most
underrated gunfights, an epic battle awash

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:20.080
in red light.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.870
Death Sentence is a badass, bloody affair
that proves Rotten Tomatoes can sometimes

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:25.500
get it wrong.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:32.300
2016's Gods of Egypt was lambasted for its
Eurocentric cast, cheesy acting, and shabby

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:32.800
CGI.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.580
But it's over-the-top performances and bizarre
look should actually make it worth your while.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.320
Set in ancient Egypt, the plot follows a human
thief who's caught in a struggle between two

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:43.820
larger-than-life deities.

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:49.040
This mythological conflict takes our characters
into outer space before plunging them into

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:49.660
the underworld.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:54.520
While the movie is absolutely bonkers when
it comes to the visuals, it also somehow still

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.140
plays everything perfectly straight.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.240
There's no cynical winking or annoying irony,
so if you enjoy big-hearted adventures, then

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.300
Gods of Egypt will be good for your soul.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:18.180
It's easy to see why Hansel and Gretel: Witch
Hunters has such a low Tomatometer.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.240
It's mostly because of Hansel and Gretel.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:24.700
They don't have character arcs, their dialogue
is flat, and there's nothing distinguishing

00:09:24.709 --> 00:09:28.990
them from each other besides the fact that
one is played by Jeremy Renner and the other

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:30.180
by Gemma Arterton.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:33.920
Really, they're just two badasses who run
around and blow up witches.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:35.680
"Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:41.840
Well, whatever you do, don't eat the candy."

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:46.180
But if you're into seeing evil creatures getting
their heads cut off, then Hansel and Gretel:

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.840
Witch Hunters is the movie for you.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:53.960
This 2013 horror adventure is like Army of
Darkness on crack, complete with anachronistic

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:58.460
weapons, buckets of blood, and more wicked
old hags than you can shake a broomstick at.

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:03.350
The monster makeup is on point, and the flying
effects are both creepy and cool.

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:08.370
And while Renner and Arterton aren't doing
a lot with their characters, Famke Janssen

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.280
more than makes up for it as the villainous
head witch.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.760
She's big, she's bad, she's over-the-top glorious.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.340
And Peter Stormare plays a sheriff, bringing
the smarm for everyone to hate.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:23.980
Granted, watching Renner punch women in the
face is a bit unsettling, but for the most

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.380
part, the kills here are creative.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:29.600
If you're in the mood for a movie that's lost
its mind, this witchy flop is for you.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:34.840
"Oh my God…"

